Research Background and Problem Statement

In today’s rapidly growing urban environment, a large number of students, bachelors, and working professionals live away from their homes for education and employment. These individuals often struggle to find healthy, affordable, and nutritious homemade food. Most rely on restaurants, fast food outlets, hostels, or mess services, which may not always provide the quality, hygiene, or emotional comfort associated with home-cooked meals.

At the same time, many homemakers possess excellent cooking skills and prepare high-quality meals every day but have limited opportunities to earn income from their talents. Family responsibilities, childcare, and household commitments often prevent them from pursuing traditional employment opportunities outside the home. As a result, their skills remain underutilized despite a growing demand for homemade food.

Additionally, many students and bachelors seek flexible part-time earning opportunities to support their education and living expenses. Traditional part-time jobs often require fixed working hours, making them difficult to balance with academic schedules. These individuals need a flexible way to earn income without affecting their studies.

HomeShare aims to address all three challenges through a single digital platform.

The platform enables verified homemakers and home cooks to sell homemade meals directly from their kitchens, allowing them to earn income while working from home. Customers can discover healthy, affordable, and trustworthy homemade food prepared by verified sellers. Students and bachelors can join the platform as delivery partners (Walkers), earning money through flexible delivery opportunities in their free time.

To ensure trust and safety, HomeShare incorporates KYC verification, seller and walker approval processes, review systems, and a Trust Score framework. By connecting food providers, customers, and delivery partners within a community-driven ecosystem, HomeShare promotes healthy eating, local entrepreneurship, women’s empowerment, student employment, and community development.

The project aims to create a sustainable and scalable platform that benefits customers, homemakers, students, and local communities while strengthening the homemade food economy.
